  it need not be the name of anything in the input.

THE LIMITATION

  Per-class provenance for overwritten classes could not be established with
  this frontend's output.

  The frontend's directory walk is not ordered by this run, and its overwrite
  report names the destination class rather than the JAR that supplied the
  surviving definition. So for any overwritten class, which input won is not
  measurable from what the frontend emitted. NO WINNER MAP APPEARS ANYWHERE IN
  THIS FILE, and none is inferred from the containment tables: containment says
  which archives hold an entry of that name, which is a different question and
  is labelled as such wherever it is used.

  What is reproducible instead is the input set itself. The ordered staging
  manifest in the declared dependency fixes it byte for byte - the same files,
  the same names, the same digests, in the same order - so the input to any
  future frontend run is determined even though the outcome of a given collision
  is not.

  Where a specific collision matters to a conclusion, the way to settle it is to
  query the graph for that class and report what is there, not to infer a
  winner. The case most likely to be misread as a coverage problem is the
  connect-shims artifact, which ships stub SparkConf, SparkContext and RDD
  classes that core and the SQL modules also ship. Table F measures what that
  looks like in THIS input set: each of the three classes was overwritten
  exactly once and is contained in exactly two archives, both of them module
  core's own shaded and pre-shade pair - because the two spark-connect-shims
  archives were excluded from this input set before the frontend ran, by the
  runbook instruction recorded in the invocation record. So in this graph the
  three-way shims collision did not occur at all, and the absence of a shims
  coverage verdict is an input-set fact rather than a graph defect. This run's
  own inventory stages both shims archives, which is one of the things
  divergence D2 changes.

