              provisioning invocation reproduced in PHASE 2. This run did not
              invoke the frontend and did not write, replace or write through the
              graph path.

  why it was not repaired. Repairing it means invoking the frontend, and the
  only output path at which both names for the graph would resolve to this run's
  bytes is the host-global one that the environment marks shared and read-only,
  that up to 45 concurrent clones read, and that the instructions forbid
  rebuilding in as many words. Replacing those bytes while a sibling clone is
  part-way through a load would corrupt that sibling's scan, and it is not
  reversible. Writing to any other path would leave $HARNESS_CPG - which
  harness/bin/run-joern.sh resolves and loads at STEP 29 - on the bytes
  measured in PHASE 3, so this file would then carry the identity of a graph no
  later stage opens. AAP 0.8.1 settles which way that tension resolves: report
  the condition, do not repair it silently, and never install, rebuild or
  overwrite to clear it.

  what it does and does not compromise. It does NOT compromise the identity in
  PHASE 3: those are the bytes the Stage 3 runner and the Stage 5 probe will
  load, measured from the file itself, so no downstream stage is verifying
  against a graph nobody has - which is the specific failure this record exists
  to prevent. It DOES mean the graph's node counts belong to the invocation in
  PHASE 2 rather than to a build over this run's own 191-archive input set, and
  that the two frontend metrics in PHASE 4 are that invocation's. Re-measuring
  the graph's method, type-declaration and file counts by loading it is the
  verification load's business, not this file's; this file measures the bytes.

  disposition: reported, not repaired. Nothing was installed, rebuilt, trimmed,
               overwritten or averaged to clear it.

